The message has nothing to do with the fountain; it was about your
dog who was confused by not having got enough to eat. Give him the
chance to kill and eat monsters. A confused pet dog might bite you
(before he starves).

But the statements of the other posters must be contrasted...
Leave the fountains alone. Or better: Leave the fountains alone!!!

While it is, literally, correct that good and bad things may happen
you should be aware that the bad things most likely kill you (or if
you're lucky, just spoil your game and thus kill you a little later).
Now say that in 50% of all cases something good happens, that means
that you either die on the first outcome or on the second. (In cases
that nothing happens there's no real reason to drink.)

The advanced players here seem to agree that a) in the beginning it
is a bad move to drink from fountains, and b) later, when you can
handle the bad fountain effects, the advantages are not necessary
any more for your success. (Though when your character will have got
tougher he may exploit even some of the bad fountain effects.)

There are two (or three) specific uses for fountains; later in the
game you may want to dilute scrolls and potions, or you may want to
remove curses (if you have no other means to do so), or as a specific
action for certain characters to obtain a specific artifact weapon.

Don't drink from fountains.

